      "id": "9oczren",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Cyclone – Concept and Dynamics"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Cyclones are low-pressure systems characterized by inward spiraling winds moving counter-clockwise in the Northern Hemisphere and clockwise in the Southern Hemisphere.",
        "Tropical cyclones derive their primary energy from the latent heat of condensation released by rising moist air over warm ocean waters.",
        "The 'Eye' of a tropical cyclone is a central area of calm, clear skies, and lowest barometric pressure, surrounded by the intense eye wall.",
        "Anticyclones are high-pressure systems featuring outward spiraling winds that bring calm, clear, and stable atmospheric conditions.",
        "Unlike tropical cyclones, temperate cyclones are frontally developed systems originating in mid-and-high latitudes driven by temperature contrasts."
      ],
      "id": "chobp5y"
    },
